  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1154 AM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nCloud cover continues to gradually increase as a shortwave\ntrough over the Red River Valley slowly lifts north. This trough\nis forecast to attempt to phase with a second trough digging\nsoutheast into the northern Plains. As this northern trough\nmoves into the central Plains tonight into Wednesday, this\nsystem is expected to become a closed upper-low centered near \nour area. In the meantime, light winds and chilly temperatures \nin the 30s continue this afternoon. \n\nAs the trough nears and begins to close off later this evening,\nflurries and snow showers begin to develop as weak PVA and WAA \noverspreads the area ahead of the northern trough. The flurries \nand light snow slowly spread southeast through tonight into \nWednesday morning. The best chance of snow (15-30%) is confined \nto an area along and north of a line from Hartington to Wayne to\nOnawa in our forecast area with a higher (30-60%) chance of \nsnow closer to the Sioux City area.\n\nThe limiting factor for snowfall is low-level dry air. This will\ngreatly limit what can reach the ground. This dry airmass is\nshallower across northeast Nebraska and western Iowa where snow\nchances are higher. The dry airmass becomes more pronounced the\nfurther south you go. Despite this, a light dusting of snow may\nbe possible across the far northeast portions of the area \nincreasing to 1-2 inches near Sioux City. Elsewhere, flurries \nare not expected to result in much, if any, accumulation. Little\nto no travel impacts are expected as a result, though if you \nare driving north of the area towards Sioux City/Falls, minor \ntravel impacts are more likely. \n\nTemperatures should warm into the upper 30s and low 40s by\nWednesday afternoon, quickly eliminating what little snow we may\nhave received. Peaks of sunshine may appear by Wednesday \nevening as surface high pressure begins to settle into the area.\nThanksgiving day appears sunny, cool, and quiet across our neck\nof the woods with temperatures in the 40s. That being said, the\npreviously mentioned closed-low will remain just east of the \nregion. This is expected to result in accumulating snow across \nportions of the Midwest. If you are traveling south and east for\nThanksgiving, travel impacts may be possible. Please plan \naccordingly due to this potential. \n\nThe end of the week sees weak ridging overspread the region.\nTemperatures should increase into the upper 40s to low 50s as a\nresult. Cloud cover begins to increase Friday evening as our\nnext trough approaches the area. Heading into the weekend, \ntemperatures should continue to be in the upper 40s to low 50s \neach afternoon. A few showers are possible (15-30%) Saturday \nafternoon into Sunday morning as a fast moving trough moves \nacross the area. Limited moisture will keep any precipitation \nlight. Temperatures remain mild into next week as largely zonal\nupper-level flow appears likely. With this progressive pattern,\nour next major weather system remains uncertain at this \njuncture with quick hitting systems appearing more likely.",
